  "leaves_out": [
    {"t": "A model of the mechanism, not a forecast", "x": "Its numbers follow from its settings. {{c:guess}} of the {{c:drawn}} unknowns that are drawn are guesses."},
    {"t": "The Opus price ratio is a price per token used as a price per call", "x": "The cost unit is one cheap-model call. The price ratios are per token, so they equal the per-call ratio only if calls are the same size in every tier. On this PC's records an Opus call averaged {{n:about 222,000|M}} cache-read tokens and a Sonnet call {{n:about 440,000|M}}. On that data the per-call ratio would be near one, while the model fixes it at two. This setting decides the comparison with the strong model directly: at one, the cost ratio at best values is {{g:ct_last_m1}} instead of {{g:ct_last}}; the middle of many runs is {{n:1.17|G}} instead of {{n:0.90|G}}; with the ratio drawn between one and two, the middle is {{n:1.01|G}}. Against the ladder it moves by about {{n:0.01|G}}."},
    {"t": "The ladder keeps no notes at all", "x": "The two ways of working without the library have no memory of any kind. Today's practice already has CLAUDE.md, the memory index, pushed notes and checks in the shared test run, and the walks show those channels doing part of the job. The model has no way of working with them. So 'against the ladder' measures the library against no memory, not against today's notes. The model cannot size the difference."},
    {"t": "Chosen cases, not a sample of work", "x": "The {{c:cases}} cases were chosen because something went wrong. They show which routes exist and where steps break. They never show how often."},
    {"t": "Case statuses are judgments, partly against an earlier design", "x": "Works, strains and breaks were set by the agents that walked the cases from the records. Another reader could set some differently. The walks counted a step as working whenever the owner's earlier knowledge-store design had a mechanism for it: {{c:ks_steps}} of the {{c:steps_total}} step judgments name that design, including {{c:ks_works}} of the {{c:works_cells}} 'works' cells. This touches the case grid, not the model's numbers."},
    {"t": "One size of work", "x": "Every piece of work is treated as about ten cheap calls. Real work varies far more in size, and a large piece is not the same as many small ones."},
    {"t": "Time and attention are not counted", "x": "Tests at the gate cost nothing. Waiting, wall-clock time and the owner's attention are left out. A lookup counts as calls; a mechanical check, carrying an entry and a handoff cost cheap-call equivalents but count as no calls."},
    {"t": "Pieces of work are independent", "x": "A piece's kind, what the cheap model would do, what the library holds and whether a look-alike exists are combined by multiplying their chances. Real work clusters: a hard problem may also be the one the library lacks."},
    {"t": "Averages, not one library", "x": "The two stocks change by their expected amounts each period. A real library has one history, with luck in it. Each period is worked out on the stocks at its start, and staleness acts on those starting stocks. So nothing written during a period helps that same period."},
    {"t": "Entries are all-or-nothing", "x": "A good entry is good wherever it applies, and a bad one bad. Partly right shows up only as the composite 'some parts' result."},
    {"t": "One pool of entries", "x": "Projects are not separated. Facts that differ between projects act only through the look-alike and wrong-entry chances."},
    {"t": "An entry acts only at the cheap attempt", "x": "A wrong accepted entry acts only at the cheap attempt. It does not follow the work up the ladder. A good entry does not help the cheap retry or Opus either. At best values {{n:0.1%|G}} of work reaches Opus with a good entry already used, so this barely moves the result."},
    {"t": "A fixed ladder and one gate", "x": "One cheap retry, then Opus, then Fable, in that order. Real sessions skip rungs, repeat them, or end with the owner. The chance the gate catches a wrong output does not depend on which model made it. The walks went another way: {{c:rung_id}}, 'hard problems pay every rung', was contradicted in {{c:rung_contra}} families, because no case climbed that way. Step 8 was 'not reached' in {{c:step8_not_reached}} cases and 'works' in {{c:step8_works}}. In {{c:not_catcher_cases}} cases ({{c:not_catcher_id}}) the higher tier was not the one that caught the error."},
    {"t": "Bad entries stay bad, keep being found, and take lookups from good ones", "x": "A bad entry turns good only when a found escape is repaired. Staleness moves good entries to bad, never back. Bad entries never fade from what a lookup returns. And the bad share is counted against the coverage that only good entries build, so a bad entry takes lookups away from good ones as well as adding look-alikes. At best values the bad share of the library reaches {{g:bad_share_last}} by the last period. In separate tests: without the lookup-taking it is {{n:0.17|G}}; over {{n:36|G}} periods as built it is {{n:0.69|G}}; over {{n:36|G}} periods without the lookup-taking and with fading it is {{n:0.04|G}}. Loop R1 rests on this choice. The cost ratio against the ladder moves by about {{n:0.01|G}} at most in these tests, because the library is used so little."},
    {"t": "Two simplifications favour the library", "x": "The mechanical check is perfect: it accepts every good entry and rejects every wrong one. Repair costs nothing beyond the rework, which an unrepaired escape also pays. Both are small at best values: a check that passes {{n:30%|G}} of wrong entries moves the cost ratio against the ladder from {{n:1.035|G}} to {{n:1.036|G}}, and repair that costs one Opus write moves it by less than {{n:0.001|G}}."},
    {"t": "Two settings are fixed", "x": "The number of periods is a chosen horizon, never drawn. The Opus price ratio has a range of one value, so the runs never vary it. It is the setting that decides the comparison with the strong model directly."},
    {"t": "Ranges are drawn evenly and on their own", "x": "If two unknowns move together in reality, the runs do not know it. This leans against runs where many unknowns favour the library at once: tied together, the share of runs better than the ladder goes from {{n:0.0%|G}} to {{n:13.6%|G}} at half strength and {{n:24.9%|G}} at full strength. The ranking measures steady rise or fall inside the ranges. It misses an effect that rises and then falls."},
    {"t": "Some limitations the model cannot show", "x": "{{c:noparam}} limitations cannot be shown by the model: {{c:noparam_ids}}. {{c:partly}} more are shown only in part: {{c:partly_ids}}. Each card says why."},
    {"t": "Some tags are generous", "x": "Several figures tagged M or S were measured on a different unit than the setting they stand for; each slider's source says how. The Opus price ratio is tagged G for that reason. One outside figure supports two settings in a row: the chance a wrong entry is accepted, and the chance it then spoils the work."},
    {"t": "Two guards on impossible draws", "x": "If a draw makes 'all parts' plus 'some parts' exceed certainty, both are scaled down in proportion; the three ceilings on what the library can hold likewise. With the current ranges neither happens."},
    {"t": "The step match is drawn by hand", "x": "The walkthroughs' ten steps and the model's stages are matched by a table, shown on the map. A limitation is placed on the map through that table and through its unknowns."}
  ],
